  Each gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. We brainstorm #1 properly now; the rest stay sketches until
  their turn.

  Before I can ask useful questions about the task model, though, I need the one thing that decides its shape.
  Notifications only mean "someone changed a task" if more than one person can change it. If this is a personal list,
  "notification" means something completely different — a due-date reminder, a nudge — and that's a different feature
  with a different design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m who else can touch a task.

  - [1mTeam tool[0m — tasks have assignees and watchers; notifications are collaboration events ("Alice reassigned your
    task"). Requires accounts, a server, and auth before step 4 works at all. This is the reading where your original
    request lands most naturally.
  - [1mPersonal tool[0m — one person's list; "notifications" become time-based reminders and due dates. No server strictly
    needed, no auth, much smaller. Rules out the "users, plural" framing entirely.
  - [1mPersonal now, team later[0m — build local-first, design the task model so an owner field and sync can be added.
    Cheapest start, but the migration is real work and half-planned multi-user tends to get rebuilt rather than
    extended.

  I recommend committing to [1mteam tool[0m if that's genuinely where this is going. It's the expensive-to-reverse axis, and
  retrofitting identity onto a single-user model is the migration people regret.
